#!/bin/sh
# test/harness_lib - harness for mux's shell tests (test/*.t), sourced by each
# of them.
#
# THE SHEBANG IS FOR THE LINTER, not for execution: this file is sourced and
# never run. It was the ONLY sourced shell file in the tree without one, and
# that made it the only one a shebang-based selector could not see, which is
# how it silently dropped out of `test/lint.t` the moment that selector stopped
# keying on `.sh`. Every lib under libexec/ already carries one.
#
# THE FOUR REPOS ALREADY ON THIS NAME CARRY NO SHEBANG HERE, and mux differing
# is deliberate rather than drift: none of them has a shellcheck test, so no
# selector of theirs can go stale and the question never arose. mux has one, and
# a shebang on a sourced file is a comment.
#
# Sets HERE (the repo root, so a test sources libexec/<lib>.sh), a private
# scratch dir T (removed on exit), a HOME pinned inside it, and fail/pass. A
# test sets _name, sources this, then the library under test. Pure string logic;
# nothing outside T is touched. POSIX sh; run one test with `sh test/<name>.t`
# or all with test/run.
HERE=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)

# T IS CANONICAL, and that is a portability fix rather than tidiness. mux
# RESOLVES a project root before recording it, correctly: a root stored once per
# symlink would make one project several. So every assertion comparing what mux
# reports against a `$T`-relative string is comparing a resolved path with an
# unresolved one, and they differ the moment TMPDIR contains a symlink.
#
# WHICH IS macOS, ALWAYS: `/var` is a symlink to `/private/var` there and
# `$TMPDIR` lives under it, so `mktemp -d` hands back `/var/folders/...` while
# mux resolves `/private/var/folders/...`. Three tests failed on the runner for
# this and no other reason (mux-new, mux-migrate, mux-discover), reading as
# defects in `mux go`, `mux new` and the discovery map.
#
# REPRODUCED ON LINUX before fixing, by pointing TMPDIR at a symlink
# (`ln -s /tmp /var/tmp/tdlink`): the same three tests fail with the same
# messages, which is what turned the macOS log from an inference into a
# measurement. Pin the ROOT the paths derive from, the same lesson HOME=$T/home
# and TMUX_TMPDIR already record here.
T=$(CDPATH= cd -- "$(mktemp -d)" && pwd -P)
trap 'rm -rf "$T"' EXIT INT TERM

# t_trap 'COMMAND': a test's OWN cleanup, composed with the scratch removal
# above instead of replacing it.
#
# SIX TESTS HAD REPLACED IT, and POSIX sh has no trap stack, so each of them
# leaked its whole scratch directory into /tmp on every run, forever. Invisible
# until TMUX_TMPDIR below put a recognisable `tmux/` inside each one; a count of
# /tmp/tmp.*/tmux then said exactly which. Same shape as the socket leak this
# suite already fixed once: the litter was uniform, so nobody could see it.
#
# test/lint.t refuses a bare `trap ... EXIT` in a test now, so the next one
# cannot reintroduce it: the rule rather than the vigilance.
t_trap() {
  trap "$1"'; rm -rf "$T"' EXIT INT TERM
}

# HOME IS PINNED INSIDE T, and that one line is what makes the claim above
# ("nothing outside T is touched") actually true rather than aspirational.
#
# It was aspirational, and got caught: every location mux derives is
# $HOME-relative by default, and each test pinned the ones it KNEW about
# ($MUX_DIR, $MUX_CACHE) by name. When the session set moved to $MUX_STATE
# (~/.local/state/mux) no existing test had any reason to know the variable
# existed, so the suite wrote four fixture session files into the REAL state
# directory and three tests went red for the wrong reason.
#
# Pinning the variables one at a time would have fixed those three tests and
# left the next new location exposed. Pinning HOME fixes every location mux will
# ever derive, including ones nobody has written yet, which is the only version
# of this that cannot rot. A test that needs a real path still has $HERE.
# The REAL home, captured before the pin. A test that needs to READ something
# optional the user installed (the indicator's venv, say) has no other way
# to find it once HOME points into T. Reading is all it is for: the pin's
# guarantee is that nothing is WRITTEN outside T, and that still holds.
HOME_REAL=$HOME
export HOME_REAL
HOME=$T/home
export HOME
mkdir -p "$HOME"

# AND THE tmux SOCKET DIRECTORY, for the same reason and by the same rule: it is
# the one location mux reaches that is NOT $HOME-relative. tmux derives it from
# TMUX_TMPDIR (default /tmp), so a test that writes a scratch tmux.conf and then
# causes anything to run `tmux source-file` reaches THE DEVELOPER'S OWN LIVE
# SERVER on the default socket: sourcing a config from a directory that is
# about to be deleted. That became reachable the moment `setup.sh install`
# learned to reload live servers, and setup.t writes exactly such a conf.
#
# It also stops the three tests that drive a real tmux leaving anything behind
# in the shared /tmp/tmux-$(id -u), where 123 dead sockets once accumulated from
# a single day of runs.
TMUX_TMPDIR=$T/tmux
export TMUX_TMPDIR
mkdir -p "$TMUX_TMPDIR"
chmod 700 "$TMUX_TMPDIR"

# AND `TMUX` HAS TO GO, which is the half that pinning a directory does not
# cover and the half that actually bit. A tmux CLIENT takes its socket from
# `$TMUX` when that is set and ignores TMUX_TMPDIR entirely, and the suite is
# normally run BY A HUMAN INSIDE tmux, so every `tmux` a test invokes was
# talking to the developer's own live server. Proven the hard way while adding
# the reload case below: a test's `tmux new-session -d -s reloadme` created a
# real session on the live server, `set-option -g status-right` overwrote the
# real status bar, and the assertions then passed by reading that server back.
# The mutation SURVIVED, because the leftover session made the second run skip
# the whole block: a test that pollutes real state also stops testing.
#
# Same rule as the HOME pin one paragraph up, with the lesson that a derived
# path is not the only way out of the sandbox: an inherited HANDLE is another.
# A test that wants $TMUX sets it to a fake value itself, and they all do.
unset TMUX TMUX_PANE

# The session set, pinned explicitly as well. HOME above already keeps it inside
# T, but a fixture is easier to read as $T/state/sessions.global than as
# $T/home/.local/state/mux/sessions.global, and it mirrors how each test pins
# $MUX_CACHE. The HOME pin is the guard; this is the convenience.
MUX_STATE=$T/state
export MUX_STATE
mkdir -p "$MUX_STATE"
_name=${_name:-$(basename -- "$0")}
# MUX_T_SAID records that a verdict was reached, so a test with an EXIT trap
# can tell "I already reported" from "I died silently". Under `set -e` any
# failing command takes a file down with no output at all, and the runner can
# then only say WHICH file went quiet, never where. A test that drives
# something contended (a real tmux) can use this to name the step it died on.
fail() { MUX_T_SAID=1; printf 'FAIL %s: %s\n' "$_name" "$1" >&2; exit 1; }
pass() { MUX_T_SAID=1; printf 'ok   %s\n' "$_name"; exit 0; }

# agent_rec FILE STATE PANE EPOCH SESSION [NOTIF]: write ONE per-pane
# agent-state record, in the current format, to FILE.
#
#     state window pane epoch notif SESSION
#
# The session comes LAST so a name containing a space reads back whole with a
# single `read -r _st _w _p _e _nid _ss` (see libexec/mux-agent-state_lib).
# notif is `-` when absent, never empty: an empty field collapses into the
# whitespace run and shifts every field after it.
#
# CENTRALISED BECAUSE IT WAS NOT. When the session name moved to the last
# field, the fixtures were hand-written in four test files and two were missed.
# One went red on main and stayed red; the other kept PASSING, because its
# stale record parsed as a session literally named `x` and the assertion only
# counted sessions, so it agreed with a format it no longer used. A fixture
# that encodes the format independently of the code is a test that can drift
# without going red, which is the failure this whole suite exists to prevent.
# One writer here means the next format change breaks compilation, not silence.
agent_rec() {   # FILE STATE PANE EPOCH SESSION [NOTIF]
  mkdir -p -- "$(dirname -- "$1")"
  printf '%s 0 %s %s %s %s\n' "$2" "$3" "$4" "${6:--}" "$5" >"$1"
}

# tmux_fresh_socket [PREFIX] -> a socket name this test run has not used.
#
# NEVER REUSE A SOCKET YOU HAVE KILLED. `tmux kill-server` returns to the
# CLIENT before the server has finished exiting, so killing a socket and
# immediately starting a session on the SAME name races the teardown: the new
# client connects to the dying server and gets "server exited unexpectedly".
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-26, and the window is not narrow: 30 failures in 60 tight
# kill-then-create cycles, failing on every SECOND iteration because a failed
# create leaves no server for the next kill to race. With a fresh name each
# time, 0 in 60.
#
# This is what made mux-undo-pane.t flake under the full suite and never alone:
# its build() killed and recreated one socket four times per run, and load
# widens the window. It cost two no-verdict failures before being chased.
#
# The caller kills the old server FIRE AND FORGET and must not wait for it.
# Waiting is the part that cannot be done: the socket FILE outlives the server
# by seconds (checked, still present after 2s), so it is not the signal either.
_tmux_sock_n=0
tmux_fresh_socket() {   # [prefix]
  _tmux_sock_n=$((_tmux_sock_n + 1))
  _tmux_sock_last=${1:-muxt}$$-$_tmux_sock_n
  printf '%s' "$_tmux_sock_last"
}

# tmux_drop_socket [NAME]: kill that server AND remove the socket file.
#
# THE FILE OUTLIVES THE SERVER, which is the same fact that forced fresh names
# in the first place: `kill-server` returns before the server is gone and the
# socket lingers for seconds afterwards. Fresh names dodge the RACE and do
# nothing about the LITTER, so every run of the three tests that drive a real
# tmux left its sockets behind for good. Measured: 123 dead ones in
# /tmp/tmux-$(id -u) against 2 live servers, from a single day of test runs,
# and 247 had been swept by hand once before.
#
# mux's own rule, turned on its own suite: mux clears the litter mux makes.
# The name is never reused, so removing the file cannot race anything.
tmux_drop_socket() {   # [name]
  _ds=${1:-${_tmux_sock_last:-}}
  [ -n "$_ds" ] || return 0
  tmux -L "$_ds" kill-server 2>/dev/null || true
  rm -f "${TMUX_TMPDIR:-/tmp}/tmux-$(id -u)/$_ds" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# --- a real PTY, on either userland ------------------------------------------
# T_PTY says which `script(1)` this box has, and is EMPTY when there is none or
# it is unusable. t_pty runs a command under a real pty, writing the typescript
# to $1 and passing the child's output through on stdout, so a caller can read
# either.
#
# WHY THIS IS A HELPER AND NOT SEVEN CALL SITES: `script -qc CMD FILE` is the
# GNU util-linux spelling, and BSD script has NO `-c` at all: its form is
# `script -q FILE command args...`, with the file FIRST and no shell. Seven
# sites across three files used the GNU form, so `mux help palette`, `mux sane`
# and latch's wait spinner ALL reported a false failure on macOS: "nothing was
# captured from the pty run" and "the palette is missing the system 0-15 band"
# are both this, not a product defect. Fix the class, not the instances.
#
# FEATURE-DETECTED, NOT KEYED ON `uname`: the question is whether this script
# takes `-c`, and asking it directly cannot be wrong about a third userland.
#
# A FUNCTION, AND CALLED ONCE AT SOURCE TIME, so a test can re-run it against a
# curated PATH. Without that the bsd arm below would be DARK on every box that
# has GNU script, which is every box here: a seam stubbed at its caller leaves
# the seam itself untested, and this suite has paid for that three times.
t_pty_detect() {
  T_PTY=
  command -v script >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  if script -q -c true /dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    T_PTY=gnu
  elif script -q /dev/null true >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    T_PTY=bsd
  fi
}
t_pty_detect

t_pty() {   # <typescript-file> <command string>
  _tp_f=$1; _tp_c=$2
  case $T_PTY in
  gnu) script -q -c "$_tp_c" "$_tp_f" ;;
  # A SHELL IS EXPLICIT HERE, because BSD script execs the command directly
  # rather than handing it to a shell, so a command STRING (which every caller
  # passes, some with a `;` in it) needs one.
  bsd) script -q "$_tp_f" /bin/sh -c "$_tp_c" ;;
  *)   return 127 ;;
  esac
}
